\fs96 \cf2 Modules and packages\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo provides a packaging system that allows you to organize and compile code libraries into importable files. This page introduces the necessary concepts about how to organize your code into modules and packages (which is a lot like Python), and shows you how to create a packaged binary with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/package/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 mojo package}} command.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Mojo modules\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To understand Mojo packages, you first need to understand Mojo modules. A Mojo module is a single Mojo source file that includes code suitable for use by other files that import it. For example, you can create a module to define a struct such as this one:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 mymodule.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 MyPair\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  first\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  second\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 fn\cf13  \cf16 __init__\cf5 (out\cf13  self\cf5 ,\cf13  first\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ,\cf13  second\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf13 first \cf5 =\cf13  first\cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf13 second \cf5 =\cf13  second\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 fn\cf13  \cf16 dump\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf13 self\cf5 .\cf13 first\cf5 ,\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 second\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Notice that this code has no 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function, so you can't execute 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mymodule.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . However, you can import this into another file with a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function and use it there.\
For example, here's how you can import 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  into a file named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that's in the same directory as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mymodule.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 main.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf13  mymodule \cf5 import\cf13  MyPair\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 fn\cf13  \cf16 main\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  mine \cf5 =\cf13  MyPair\cf5 (\cf19 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf19 4\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     mine\cf5 .\cf13 dump\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Alternatively, you can import the whole module and then access its members through the module name. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 main.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 import\cf13  mymodule\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 fn\cf13  \cf16 main\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  mine \cf5 =\cf13  mymodule\cf5 .\cf13 MyPair\cf5 (\cf19 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf19 4\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     mine\cf5 .\cf13 dump\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also create an alias for an imported member with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 as
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 main.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 import\cf13  mymodule \cf5 as\cf13  my\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 fn\cf13  \cf16 main\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  mine \cf5 =\cf13  my\cf5 .\cf13 MyPair\cf5 (\cf19 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf19 4\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     mine\cf5 .\cf13 dump\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this example, it only works when 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mymodule.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is in the same directory as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Currently, you can't import 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  files as modules if they reside in other directories. That is, unless you treat the directory as a Mojo package, as described in the next section.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 A Mojo module may include a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 main()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  function and may also be executable, but that's generally not the practice and modules typically include APIs to be imported and used in other Mojo programs.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Mojo packages\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A Mojo package is just a collection of Mojo modules in a directory that includes an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file. By organizing modules together in a directory, you can then import all the modules together or individually. Optionally, you can also compile the package into a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .mojopkg
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file that's easier to share and still compatible with other system architectures.\
You can import a package and its modules either directly from source files or from a compiled 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .mojopkg
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file. It makes no real difference to Mojo which way you import a package. When importing from source files, the directory name works as the package name, whereas when importing from a compiled package, the filename is the package name (which you specify with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/package/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 mojo package}} command\'97it can differ from the directory name).\
For example, consider a project with these files:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 main.mojo\cb1 \
\cb15 mypackage/\cb1 \
\cb15     __init__.mojo\cb1 \
\cb15     mymodule.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mymodule.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is the same code from examples above (with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct) and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is empty.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 __init__.mojo
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  file is essential. If you don't have it, Mojo won't recognize the directory as a package and you can't import 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 mymodule
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In this case, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file can now import 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  through the package name like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 main.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf13  mypackage\cf5 .\cf13 mymodule \cf5 import\cf13  MyPair\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 fn\cf13  \cf16 main\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  mine \cf5 =\cf13  MyPair\cf5 (\cf19 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf19 4\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     mine\cf5 .\cf13 dump\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This immediately works:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 mojo main.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 2 4\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 However, if you don't want the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mypackage
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  source code in the same location as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you can compile it into a package file like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 mojo package mypackage \cf19 -o\cf13  mypack.mojopkg\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 A 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 .mojopkg
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  file contains non-elaborated code, so you can share it across systems. The code becomes an architecture-specific executable only after it's imported into a Mojo program that's then compiled with 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 mojo build
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Now, you can move the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mypackage
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  source somewhere else, and the project files now look like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 main.mojo\cb1 \
\cb15 mypack.mojopkg\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Because we named the package 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mypack
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , we need to fix the import statement:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 main.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf13  mypack\cf5 .\cf13 mymodule \cf5 import\cf13  MyPair\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 And the code works the same:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 mojo main.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 2 4\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 If you want to rename your package, you cannot simply edit the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 .mojopkg
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  filename, because the package name is encoded in the file. You must instead run 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 mojo package
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  again to specify a new name.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 The 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 __init__
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  file\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As mentioned above, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file is required to indicate that a directory should be treated as a Mojo package, and it can be empty.\
Currently, top-level code is not supported in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  files, so unlike Python, you can't write code in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that executes upon import. You can, however, add structs and functions, which you can then import from the package name.\
However, instead of adding APIs in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file, you can import module members, which has the same effect by making your APIs accessible from the package name, instead of requiring the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 <package_name>.<module_name>
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  notation.\
For example, again let's say you have these files:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 main.mojo\cb1 \
\cb15 mypackage/\cb1 \
\cb15     __init__.mojo\cb1 \
\cb15     mymodule.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Let's now add the following line in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 __init__.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf13  \cf5 .\cf13 mymodule \cf5 import\cf13  MyPair\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 That's all that's in there. Now, we can simplify the import statement in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 main.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 main.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf13  mypackage \cf5 import\cf13  MyPair\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This feature explains why some members in the Mojo standard library can be imported from their package name, while others required the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 <package_name>.<module_name>
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  notation. For example, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/algorithm/functional/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 functional}} module resides in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 algorithm
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  package, so you can import members of that module (such as the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 map()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function) like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

